The `userScripts` folder contains all the core functionality for executing user-defined automation scripts across multiple languages including JavaScript, TypeScript, Lua, and Python. It provides runtime environments, variable management, terminal output bridging, and script execution handling while maintaining global state and caching mechanisms. The folder serves as the central hub for in-game automation, managing everything from script loading and execution to variable resolution and output rendering.

Every storage key here (userScripts, userGlobalScripts, userGlobalVars, userVariables) is built from a connection id that goes through canonicalConnectionId (features/connection/connectionId.ts): the DSL game and the not-connected state are both `dsl-mud`, and the legacy `default` id also resolves to `dsl-mud`. Before that, a script saved while disconnected landed in a different bucket than the one the connected client read (Globals tab blank on reopen). userScriptRuntime, runtimeSingleton, useUserScriptSandbox and the two stores each keep their own safe-id helper, so a new store must use canonicalConnectionId itself and never a literal 'default' fallback. Cloud global-script buckets uploaded as `default` are merged into `dsl-mud` in replaceGlobalScriptBuckets (dsl-mud wins a per-language conflict); old localStorage keys are folded in at start by features/connection/connectionIdMigration.ts.

Lua and Python are ONE engine per connection (decided 2026-10-30): globalRuntime owns it (luaCache / pyCache), the Globals source for the language is run into it, and runUserScript(script, api, connectionId) runs every trigger / alias / timer of that language in the same Lua state / Skulpt module namespace, so a function or variable defined anywhere is visible everywhere. Saving the Globals source for a language restarts that engine (closeLuaState / resetPythonEngine); editing a script never does. runUserScript WITHOUT a connectionId is the isolated path (plugin-bundled scripts): a Lua state that is closed afterwards, a throwaway Python namespace. Pass the connection id from every new call site or the script silently loses the shared scope. JavaScript / TypeScript scripts are NOT one program the way Lua and Python are: each is its own function with its own scope, and what they share is the Globals JS/TS exports, bound by name (see the invoke contract below). Scripts can start scripts synchronously (sendCommand -> alias), so luaRuntime keeps a per-state api stack and puts the outer run's `api` / `event` globals back (Python restores the bridge builtins the same way); a long-lived Lua state also needs its value stack reset after every run (runWithApi) or each failed load leaks its error message. pythonRuntime configures Skulpt once, runs scripts through Sk.compile + the module function in a shared globals dict instead of importMainWithBody (which resets sys.modules per run), and caches the compiled module function by source. Tests: scriptEngines.test.ts (real fengari and Skulpt).

Hot path notes: triggerOmitStore lowercases each rule needle once at compile time (needleLower) and the line at most once per call via a module-level one-entry cache (lowerOf) — rules must be set through setOmitRules/setPluginOmitRules so needleLower is filled. userScriptRuntime.triggerPassesMatch skips variable expansion entirely for match text with no { (expansion of such text is the identity); text with a token still takes the original two expansion passes, so nested-token behavior is unchanged.

The invoke contract (decided 2026-10-31): a global written in ANY language (global.<language>.<path>) is callable from a script in ANY language, with plain-data arguments and a plain-data result (plainData.ts toPlainData: functions, class instances and cycles drop to undefined). globalRuntime.invokeGlobalSync is the one implementation and returns a GlobalCall (types.ts): `settled: true` with the value when the function finished before the call returned (every Lua function, every non-async JS/TS function, any Python function that does not suspend), else `settled: false` with a never-rejecting promise; invokeGlobalById is just the awaitable form (it resolves undefined on failure, it never rejects). The api carries both runGlobal (Promise) and runGlobalNow (GlobalCall); every api builder must set BOTH (useUserScriptSandbox, userScriptRuntime) or Lua/Python lose the call-like form and fall back to callback-only. Sync-ness is deliberate and must be kept: a script must start running in the turn it was triggered in (runJavascript/runPythonInEngine do no await before the script body, getJsGlobalScope and ensureJsModule are synchronous), because existing scripts depend on ordering. Python gets this from Skulpt itself (startBound in pythonRuntime: work runs synchronously until it suspends, so it is settled at once unless it sleeps). JS/TS scripts run inside an AsyncFunction (top-level await works) and get the exports of the Globals JS + TS files as bare names (getJsGlobalScope/wrapGlobalExport: functions get the (api,args)/(args) convention applied for them and return untouched, objects are write-through Proxies, the script own language wins a clash, bridge names/keywords/non-identifiers are skipped); the script body sits in a nested function so it may redeclare a bound name. Lua/Python runGlobal(id, args, callback): returns the value when settled, else nil/None; the callback is ALWAYS called later as (result, err), never inside the calling script, and a Lua callback is dropped if its state was closed (closedStates). Lua gets JS whole numbers as integers (pushJsValue). Tests: globalContract.test.ts and scriptEngines.test.ts (real fengari and Skulpt). Mobile mirrors all of this in features/scripts (global-runtime.ts, lua-runtime.ts, python-runtime.ts, script-runtime.ts), except that mobile only uses an async script body if the engine can run async code built at run time (detectAsyncFunction); keep the two in step.
